## Proof

**Claim.** The only real-valued bounded measurable function $f$ on $[1,2]$ satisfying
$$\int_1^2 x^n f(x)\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for all } n = 0, 1, 2, \dots$$
is $f(x) = 0$ almost everywhere on $[1,2]$.

---

**Step 1. Vanishing against all polynomials.**

By linearity of the integral, the hypothesis $\int_1^2 x^n f(x)\,dx = 0$ for every $n \ge 0$ implies
$$\int_1^2 p(x)\,f(x)\,dx = 0$$
for every polynomial $p(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{N} a_k x^k$.

**Step 2. Vanishing against all continuous functions.**

By the Weierstrass approximation theorem, polynomials are dense in $C([1,2])$ with respect to the uniform norm $\|\cdot\|_\infty$. Let $g \in C([1,2])$ and let $\varepsilon > 0$. Choose a polynomial $p$ with $\|g - p\|_\infty < \varepsilon$. Since $f$ is bounded, say $|f(x)| \le M$, we have
$$\left|\int_1^2 g(x) f(x)\,dx\right| = \left|\int_1^2 (g(x)-p(x))f(x)\,dx\right| \le \varepsilon \cdot M \cdot (2-1) = \varepsilon M.$$
As $\varepsilon > 0$ is arbitrary,
$$\int_1^2 g(x) f(x)\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for all } g \in C([1,2]). \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Step 3. Vanishing against indicator functions of intervals.**

Let $[a,b] \subset [1,2]$ be a closed subinterval. For each $\varepsilon > 0$ small enough that $a - \varepsilon \ge 1$ and $b + \varepsilon \le 2$, define the continuous "tent" function
$$g_\varepsilon(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & x \in [a,b], \\ \text{linear on } [a-\varepsilon, a] \text{ rising from } 0 \text{ to } 1, \\ \text{linear on } [b, b+\varepsilon] \text{ falling from } 1 \text{ to } 0, \\ 0 & x \notin [a-\varepsilon, b+\varepsilon]. \end{cases}$$
Then $g_\varepsilon \in C([1,2])$, so by $(\ast)$, $\int_1^2 g_\varepsilon f = 0$. As $\varepsilon \to 0^+$, $g_\varepsilon \to \mathbf{1}_{[a,b]}$ pointwise, and $|g_\varepsilon| \le 1$. Since $f$ is bounded (hence integrable) and $g_\varepsilon f$ is dominated by the integrable function $|f|$, the dominated convergence theorem gives
$$\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = 0 \quad \text{for every } [a,b] \subset [1,2].$$

**Step 4. Conclusion: $f = 0$ a.e.**

Define $F(t) = \int_1^t f(x)\,dx$ for $t \in [1,2]$. Since $f$ is bounded and measurable, $F$ is (Lipschitz) continuous. Step 3 shows $F(t) = 0$ for every $t \in [1,2]$. By the Lebesgue differentiation theorem (or the fundamental theorem of calculus for the Lebesgue integral), $F$ is differentiable a.e. with $F'(t) = f(t)$ a.e. Since $F \equiv 0$, we get
$$f(x) = 0 \quad \text{almost everywhere on } [1,2].$$

Conversely, $f = 0$ a.e. clearly satisfies all the given conditions. $\blacksquare$

